{"schemaVersion":{"patch":0,"minor":3,"major":0},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["Linux"],"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"floydCycleDetection","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"<","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"genericParameter"},{"text":">(","kind":"text"},{"text":"from","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"start","kind":"internalParam"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", ","kind":"text"},{"text":"successor","kind":"externalParam"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"f","kind":"internalParam"},{"text":": (","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":") -> ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":") -> (tailLength","kind":"text"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", cycleLength","kind":"text"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Int","preciseIdentifier":"s:Si","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":", root","kind":"text"},{"text":": ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":") ","kind":"text"},{"text":"where","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":" : ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Equatable","preciseIdentifier":"s:SQ","kind":"typeIdentifier"}]}]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"text":"Discussion","type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Returns "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"tailLength"},{"type":"text","text":", the number of steps from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"start"},{"type":"text","text":" to the first vertex on the cycle (μ),"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cycleLength"},{"type":"text","text":" (λ), and that first cyclic vertex, "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"root"},{"type":"text","text":". The closure is called exactly"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"3ν + 2μ + λ times, where ν is the least multiple of λ that is at least max(μ, 1). That count"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"includes calling it on "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"start"},{"type":"text","text":" twice, once for each runner’s first step."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"brentCycleDetection(from:successor:)"},{"type":"text","text":" calls it less often."}]},{"name":"Precondition","content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"The orbit of ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"start"},{"text":" is eventually periodic (always so on a finite state","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"space). On an infinite orbit the function does not return.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}],"style":"note","type":"aside"}]}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The tail and cycle of the orbit of "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"start"},{"type":"text","text":" under "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"successor"},{"type":"text","text":", by Floyd’s algorithm (Knuth,"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"The Art of Computer Programming","type":"text"}]},{"type":"text","text":", vol. 2, §3.1, exercise 6): a tortoise and a hare at twice its"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"speed meet on the cycle; a second pass from "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"start"},{"type":"text","text":" finds the first cyclic vertex, and a third"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"lap measures the cycle. O(μ + λ) time, O(1) memory, and states need only be "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Equatable"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/functionalgraphs\/floydcycledetection(from:successor:)"]}],"sections":[],"metadata":{"roleHeading":"Function","title":"floydCycleDetection(from:successor:)","externalID":"s:16FunctionalGraphs19floydCycl